<section class="section section--tight" aria-labelledby="law-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="law-title">First, which law applies to your return?</h2>
      <p>Sources explain that what decides this is the year in which the income was earned, not the date on which you file.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="data-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Which law applies, scrolls sideways on small screens">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Income earned in</th><th scope="col">Law</th><th scope="col">What this means</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Up to 31 March 2026 (FY 2025-26 and earlier)</th><td>Income-tax Act, 1961</td><td>Old terms such as "assessment year" and old section numbers still apply to these returns</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">From 1 April 2026 (tax year 2026-27 onward)</th><td>Income-tax Act, 2025</td><td>New "tax year" term and new section numbers. Forms and dates should be checked closer to filing</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
    <p class="section-foot">We found conflicting reports on due dates and new return forms for tax year 2026-27, so we are not stating them here. Check the {% ext "https://www.incometax.gov.in/", "Income Tax Department's portal" %}, or ask your professional to show you the notification.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="bring-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="bring-title">What to have ready</h2>
      <p>Your situation decides which records matter. Start with the Annual Information Statement and tax credit statement on the portal, then gather whatever applies below.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="deliver-grid">
      <article><h3>If you are salaried</h3><p>Employer's TDS certificate, salary slips, and proof of any claims you are making, such as rent or investments.</p></article>
      <article><h3>If you run a business</h3><p>Profit and loss account and balance sheet, GST returns, bank statements, loan statements, and stock details.</p></article>
      <article><h3>If you are a professional</h3><p>Fee receipts, TDS statements from clients or hospitals, and expenses kept separate from personal ones.</p></article>
      <article><h3>If you invest</h3><p>Capital gains statements from brokers and funds, interest and dividend records, and property sale or purchase papers.</p></article>
      <article><h3>If you rent out property</h3><p>Rent received, municipal tax paid, and home loan interest details.</p></article>
      <article><h3>If you have foreign income or assets</h3><p>Tell your professional early. These need specific disclosure and care.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="choose-title">
  <div class="wrap layout-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="choose-title" class="first-h2">Choosing the right return</h2>
      <p>Different ITR forms exist for different income types. In recent years, business or professional income has usually ruled out the simplest forms, with separate forms for presumptive income and for full accounts. The wrong form can make a return defective, so ask why a form was chosen.</p>
      <p>For a business or professional, two questions often matter more than the form:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Presumptive income or full accounts?</strong> Section 58 of the 2025 Act lets eligible residents declare income on a set basis: for many businesses with turnover up to ₹2 crore, or ₹3 crore if cash receipts are within 5%; for specified professions, 50% of receipts up to ₹50 lakh, or ₹75 lakh if cash receipts are within 5%. Conditions apply, and brokers and agents are excluded from the business scheme.</li>
        <li><strong>Is a tax audit needed?</strong> Section 63 applies where business turnover exceeds ₹1 crore (₹10 crore if cash receipts and cash payments are each within 5%), where professional receipts exceed ₹50 lakh, or where you claim less than the presumptive profit in some cases. It needs an "accountant", meaning a CA in practice.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="work">What a filing engagement should include</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Collecting records and matching them with the portal's statements</li>
        <li>Working out income and tax, and advising which option they used and why</li>
        <li>Showing you the draft and tax computation</li>
        <li>Paying any balance tax, which you approve and normally pay yourself</li>
        <li>Filing and <strong>verifying</strong> the return. An unverified return may not count as filed</li>
        <li>Giving you the acknowledgement and computation, and keeping your supporting records available</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="yours">What stays with you</h2>
      <p>The return is filed in your name. Read the income figures before you verify it, and do not agree to claims that you cannot support. Be especially wary of anyone offering a bigger refund through a deduction you did not actually incur.</p>

      <h2 id="fees">What affects the fee</h2>
      <p>Number of income sources, capital gains volume, business accounts needed, foreign items, past years pending, and whether notices are covered. {% if '/guides/accounting-services-cost-in-delhi/' | built %}See <a href="/guides/accounting-services-cost-in-delhi/">what affects accounting costs</a>.{% endif %}</p>

      <h2 id="review">When to get more specialised help</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>You have received a notice. See {% routeLink "/services/income-tax-notice-handling/", "income tax notices" %}.</li>
        <li>You have large capital gains, property sales or foreign assets.</li>
        <li>You have missed years, or need to revise a return.</li>
        <li>Your turnover or receipts are near a tax audit limit.</li>
      </ul>

